==English==

===Etymology===
From the earlier form ''[[an't|an’t]]'', a contraction of “are not” and “am not” [http://www.bartleby.com/61/63/A0156300.html].  Historically, it was present in many dialects of the English language, but not that of southeast England, which became the standard.

===Pronunciation===
*{{audio|en-us-ain't.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Contraction===
'''ain’t'''

# {{dialectal}} [[are]] [[not]], [[aren't|aren’t]]; [[is]] not, [[isn't|isn’t]]; [[am]] not.
# {{context|dialectal|colloquial}} [[have]] not, [[haven't|haven’t]]; [[has]] not, [[hasn't|hasn’t]].
# {{context|colloquial|some dialects}} [[do]] not, [[don't|don’t]]; [[does]] not, [[doesn't|doesn’t]]; [[did]] not, [[didn't|didn’t]].

====Usage notes====
*This word is commonly considered an [[illiteracy]] and is heavily stigmatized, with its users often being perceived as ignorant.

===Quotations===
*'''1885''' — [[w:Gilbert and Sullivan|Gilbert & Sullivan]], ''[[s:The_Mikado|The Mikado]]''
*:We figure in lively paint -
*:Our attitude’s queer and quaint —
*:You’re wrong if you think it '''ain't''', oh!

